Hello again,

In our data-set of sperm whale acoustic localisations, we have some animals detections that I was unable to localise satisfactorily.  I believe this occurred because the whale vocalised for a short period only (perhaps we caught the end of a dive cycle) - it revealed its presence but could not be tracked for a long enough to localise using target motion analysis.  I don't believe this was a range-dependent event, but due to normal patterns of vocalisation and silence during dive-surface cycles.  It cannot contribute to the detection function, but i think it should be represented in the encounter rate.

Fortunately this was a rare event in the data, but how should it be represented in Distance - as a data row with a missing slant range; as a multiplier?  Or included with the proportion of the sperm whale population that was not vocalising and was unavailable for detection (/localisation).

Best regards,

Chris

