On Monday, October 16, 2017 at 2:53:07 AM UTC-6, David Lawrence Miller wrote:
> Hi Kevin, hi listfolk,
>
> The squareness of the segments is by no means essential for the DSM approach to work. Things to bear in mind are:
>
> - not having segments to short that you end up "binarising" your response (i.e., just 1s and 0s)
> - not making segments so long that there is little information about spatial pattern
> - enabling use of any spatial predictors at the resolution that would be meaningful (e.g., not averaging over SST such that there's so information to build a model)
>
> Hope this helps!
>
> cheers,
> --dave
>
> On 13 Oct 2017, at 20:47, hawk...@ualberta.ca wrote:
>
> > OK, so I've thought of one potential solution. I could increase the length of my transect segments (or use the entire transect) to reduce the degree of zero inflation. My surveys are multispecies, and for the species groups I am currently analyzing (songbirds and shorebirds) I am working with 250m transect segments, chosen because it is approximately double the truncation distance of my observation data (where detection < 15%), thus the segments are approximately square. The vast majority of my transects are ~ 1km long, so potentially using the entire transect could solve my response distribution problem, but I'm not sure how valid this is in density surface modeling. It would mean that for species groups with shorter truncation distances, the segments are not going to be square. How problematic would this be?
